	<tr><td style="padding: 1px 1px 0 1px;">
		<a name="msg12885"></a>
		<table cellpadding="3" cellspacing="0" border="0" width="100%">
			<tr><td class="windowbg">
				<table width="100%" cellpadding="5" cellspacing="0">
					<tr>
						<td valign="top" width="15%" rowspan="2">
							<b><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?action=profile;u=528" title="View the profile of Qadain">Qadain</a></b><br />
							<span class="smalltext">
								Guild Leader<br />
								Orc Pawn<br />
								<img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/star.gif" alt="*" border="0" /><br />
								Gender: <img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/Male.gif" alt="Male" border="0" /><br />
								Posts: 2<br />
								<br />
								<br />
								An Everquest Player on Rodcet Nife<br />
								<br />
								
								
								
								<br />
								<a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?action=profile;u=528"><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/icons/profile_sm.gif" alt="View Profile" title="View Profile" border="0" /></a>
								<a href="http://apoth.dyndns.org" title="&lt;Apotheosis&gt;" target="_blank"><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/www_sm.gif" alt="WWW" border="0" /></a>
							</span>
						</td>
						<td valign="top" width="85%" height="100%">
							<table width="100%" border="0"><tr>
								<td align="left" valign="middle"><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=1201.msg12885#msg12885"><img src="http://www.rodcetnife.org/Themes/classic/images/post/xx.gif" alt="" border="0" /></a></td>
								<td align="left" valign="middle">
									<b><a href="http://www.rodcetnife.org/index.php?topic=1201.msg12885#msg12885">Re: Christmas Contest</a></b><br />
									<span class="smalltext">&#171; <b>Reply #4 on:</b> November 30, 2004, 06:44:24 pm &#187;</span></td>
								<td align="right" valign="bottom" height="20" nowrap="nowrap" style="font-size: smaller;">
								</td>
							</tr></table>
							<hr width="100%" size="1" class="hrcolor" />
							<i>What are little girls made of?</i>  Sugar and spice and everything nice.<br /><i>What are little boys made of?</i>  Snakes and snails and puppy-dog tails.<br /><i>What is your guild made of?</i> …what is my guild &lt;Apotheosis&gt; made of?<br /><br />&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;What makes a guild?  Is it the people, who work, day in and day out, for progression, friendship, or even survival?  Is it the institution, the rules, the charter, the atmosphere that allows for the existence of everything else?  Or is it the leadership, who directs the guild and sets and example for the rest of the guild?<br /><br />&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;My answer is that a guild is not “made” of any of these things.  Yes, these are parts of a guild, and absolutely necessary parts at that, but a guild is an organic being not very unlike a person, and you wouldn’t say that your intelligence makes you, or that your strength of character makes you, or that your habits and customs make you.  You might say that your experiences and how you’ve reacted to them make you what you are.  I’d certainly say that my guild’s experiences make it what it is today.  But since the guild lacks a mouth to speak, the best I can do is to give my experience of the guild’s experiences.<br /><br />&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;If you really want to hear about it, the first thing you’ll probably want to know is how my guild was formed, what the founding members were like, and how they were occupied and all before they made the guild, and all that David Copperfield kind of crap, but I don’t feel like going into it, if you want to know the truth.(*)  Actually, the &lt;Apotheosis&gt; today is a guild restarted by Bagbokk the, ahem, dark elf shadowknight after the guild had been lying dormant for ages.  He restarted the guild almost exactly two years ago, after he returned to the game after his long break caused by the collapse of &lt;Avatars of Discord&gt;.  Some of the people that restarted the guild with him were Feilynn, Woundhealer, and Mother.  My memory’s a little lacking here, since I was only a lowly mid level 50’s enchanter in a 60+ guild.  Back then, the other guild members were like gods to me.  I came back from a lengthy break when the guild was restarted, I was in the guild only because I was in the original &lt;Apotheosis&gt;, and I felt as out of place as a level 60 would in my guild today.  <br /><br />&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;At first, I was too low level to do anything that the guild was doing, like keying for BoT, doing PoN maze, or even PoJ trials.  Back then, we had an alliance with &lt;Theft of Life&gt;, led by Vumeldar, and one of the only raids I can remember now with them is a Tunare raid.  This raid was my first raid, ever, and unfortunately, not only was I terribly under-leveled, I also didn’t want to ruin my faction!  I pretty much just watched and dragged corpses.  This was one of the longest raids I’ve ever been on, and it was a heck of a first raid to participate in.  We took many hours clearing, which for me meant many hours of dragging corpses, and at the end we were racing not only against repops, but also against an emergency server reset!  Fortunately, we killed Tunare in time, and I wasn’t completely useless during the fight: I illusioned and was part of the ogre wall that blocked the main tank from being pushed back from Tunare’s proc.  We quickly distributed loot, and I won the PoG enchanter robe that drops of Tunare, my first raid loot on my first raid.  It was a memorable day for me, and for everyone else there as well.  That raid very effectively discouraged anyone from even suggesting another Tunare raid for quite a few months.  <br /><br />&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Another memorable raid that I participated in was on was an Avatar of War raid last March.  It would have been an important and impressive kill for us, even though I wasn’t aware of its importance.  We were still raiding with &lt;Theft of Life&gt;, and I remember I was either 64 or 65, since I had just gotten the 64 charm and the Protectors of Zeks were CoD charmable.  I had heard stories of guilds competing in a cutthroat manner over Naggy or Vox or Fear in the old days, but this was my first encounter with another guild competing for a target with mine.  We cleared a great many giants—everything seemed so big to me, and I didn’t know my way around Kael, since it was my first time and the map feature hadn’t been implemented--and eventually killed the Statue of Rallos Zek, spawning the Idol.  Somewhere along the line, &lt;Warlords of Wrath&gt; set up in the corner opposite to us and “gave” us a time limit to pull the AoW or they’d pull it.  Needless to say, all of us were outraged and absolutely furious.  We pulled the Idol, but had to slow it for some reason.  The Idol’s usually fought unslowed, as you know, as a “practice” for AoW, and since we fought it slowed, I suppose &lt;Warlords of Wrath&gt; thought that a good enough reason to think we couldn’t kill AoW.  As it happened, if we had fought the Idol unslowed, I don’t think we could have met their time limit.  As it was, we weren’t completely ready by the time we were forced to pull AoW, and we wiped.  Now, if that were the end of the story, what I would have learned is that guilds compete for targets and some of them can be pretty unfair about it.  But there’s more:  a shadowknight from &lt;Theft of Life&gt; apparently ninjalooted a Ring of Destruction from WoW’s AoW kill.  There was a big controversy about this, and it must have been all over Flamevault.  The “time limit” and the ninjalooting together made this an unforgettable—not exactly in a good way—raid.  I guess the platitude “two wrongs don’t make a right” applies here, and the ninjalooter was removed from the guild.  Everyone learned their lesson; two lessons, in fact: be competitive, but not mean, and don’t retaliate against anything with foul play.  About a year later, we went back and killed the AoW, and some of us reminisced about the bad old days.<br /><br />&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;One of the strangest things ever to happen to my guild, in which one of the strangest things ever happened to me, occurred in April of last year.  &lt;Reign of Swords&gt; was killing Aerin`Dar one night, and I believe that they worked with &lt;Century of Flame&gt; on that raid.  After a couple of tries, they started losing some people, and somehow, Bagbokk convinced their leadership to let our &lt;Apotheosis&gt;-&lt;Theft of Life&gt; alliance join the raid, so it turned out to be a four guild raid on Aerin`Dar.  I don’t even remember how many people were there, maybe 60 or 70, only that it was the largest raid I had ever attended up to that time.  As I remember it, we wiped the first try and called it a night.  We tried against the next day and won.  They distributed the loot, and I won the Honorary Amulet, which was a really big deal for me, and we dropped down the chute and clicked the lightning at the end of the tunnel in Aerin`Dar’s chamber.  Then the server crashed.  I logged back on to curses and screams of lost flags from the rollback in guildchat and ooc.  I lost my loot too, and surprisingly, I wasn’t all that upset.  I was just happy that I kept my flag.  As it turned out, I soloed about 400 AAs in HoH.  What a way to learn the lesson that, as they say, “flags &gt; loot.”  <br /><br />&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;In May of last year, Bagbokk quit and handed guild leadership to me.  At the time, I was surprised at both.  The leadership of &lt;Theft of Life&gt; applied to &lt;Ascent&gt; and we absorbed the rest.  We were farming for Ssra Emp keys and not getting very far, but I don’t think that’s why Bagbokk quit.  I’m not sure exactly why he quit, but after being guild leader myself for a year and a half, I have a good list of reasons why anyone would quit.  I was surprised that he quit, but I was even more surprised that he handed guild leadership back to me.  When he restarted the guild, I wasn’t active and I was the guild leader only in name, and of course I transferred leadership to him.  When he quit, I had very little raid experience, no raid leadership experience at all, and pretty much no idea how to run a guild.  I supposed that he handed leadership back to me because I’m his brother (imagine a high elf enchanter having an ogre, I mean, dark elf shadowknight as a little brother, but it’s true). Basically, I didn’t know what to do, and we did nothing for a month.  To be honest, I didn’t know why people like Haleden, Zirithi, Musikal, Sambrosa, Dractwo, Sydnie, Mother, and Ehland stayed in the guild through that, but I’m glad they did.  It seemed like a sad place, and it was certainly a dark time for the guild.<br /><br />&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Fortunately, two changes occurred that were completely out of my control that decided the next few months of my guild’s activity.  If I were to look back on my life at a glance, it would seem like an uncanny streak of uninterrupted lucky breaks for me.  That’s obviously not the case, but it seems like whenever I was at a loss for what to do, something always happened that showed me exactly what I should do, a “sign,” so to speak.  In the first case, it was a patch, changing “alternate access” keys from keying just an individual to keying an entire group for zones like Plane of Tactics, Halls of Honor, and Crypt of Decay.  I decided that we would get keyed for Tactics, and my guild spent many, many hours farming the Plane of Storms desert for Diaku.  Most of us acquired Tactics access, and we farmed The Diaku Overseer and Glykus Helmir for a good while.  We couldn’t farm those forever, and as I wondered what to do next, another patch increased drops from BoT named, in particular, BoT tower bosses would always drop two items and have a good chance of dropping a rune.  So we farmed BoT tower bosses and the PoI dragon, Xanamech Nezmirthafen, for a month and a half.  When I say “farm,” I don’t mean to say that it was always easy.  I remember the first time we tried Auliffe Chaoswind, we wiped at 0%.  In the greater scheme of things, I suppose that farming tactics and BoT gave us an idea of what raiding and farming equipment was like, and I got an idea of what leading raids and serving as guild leader involved.<br /><br />&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Then, last August, just when we were tired of BoT towers and looking for something else to do, Riake from &lt;Century of Flame&gt; approached me with the idea of an alliance to work on PoP progression.  I agreed, and this began the “modern era” of my guild, so to speak, the era of raiding for progression.  Working together for four months, we killed Grummus, the Carprin cycle, Terris Thule, Saryrn, Manaetic Behemoth, Vallon and Tallon Zek, Aerin`Dar, and two of the three HoH trials.  We eventually got stuck on Bertoxxulous and Rydda`Dar, and many of the core &lt;Century of Flame&gt; members applied to other guilds, like &lt;Conviction&gt;.  My guild almost collapsed, then, since we depended on CoF to raid the bigger targets.  Eventually, we absorbed the rest of &lt;Century of Flame&gt;, including Kaddari, Gtex, and Carbon.  However, the person dearest to me left &lt;Century of Flame&gt; for &lt;Conviction&gt;.  People leaving for another guild had never upset me much; I understood that it’s the nature of the game and that people come and go, but this one really devastated me.  I learned, not only that worlds can fall apart and crumble very quickly, but really realized for the first time that guilds are not completely unlike revolving doors.  Some people enjoy taking the time to go ‘round and ‘round, and some people just move through as quickly as possible, and then there are those stuck who tire of getting nowhere that don’t really want to leave, but eventually do.<br /><br />&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;In January, &lt;Apotheosis&gt; was raiding alone again, this time in Ssra.  We farmed Ssra targets like Creator, Arch Lich, High Priest, Cursed, and the good old BoT tower bosses for a month and a half.  Somewhere along the line, I asked Rhonwyn if &lt;Phoenix Evolution&gt; would work with us on PoP progression.  She agreed to try it, but before we could work together on a regular basis, she resigned as guild leader.  I decided to start again with PoP, and I invited &lt;Phoenix Evolution&gt; members on our PoP progression raids.  Some of them that I can still list without even pausing were Fizwin, Keyes, Geide, Tempir, Ailicec, and Dergewinner.  About this time, I heard that &lt;Dragons Fist&gt; had killed Rydda`Dar, and we weren’t competing with them or anything, but I thought that if they could do it, so could we!  So we did, and in the next week, we killed Bertoxxulous and Mithaniel Marr.  By the first week of April we were flagged for Sol Ro’s Tower, while a month ago we were still farming Ssra.  It was a very exciting and busy time for us, and we were managing full raids and preparing to try Rallos Zek.  That one took a while.<br /><br />&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;I’m not sure what has inconvenienced my guild more, “higher” guilds recruiting my guild’s members, or raiding reflag targets that we haven’t been able to kill.  At any rate, we weren’t able to try Rallos Zek for a good month or so because it was being farmed or killed for reflags.  In the middle of June, we were gathering for a Rallos Zek raid when &lt;Union of Power&gt; was doing gathering as well.  We didn’t win that night, but I got a good look at the encounter.  We didn’t win the next time we tried it, either, due to some freak accidents that didn’t have anything to do with how well we worked together.  The third time we tried RZtW together, the &lt;Union of Power&gt; raidleader, Orche, who had led the previous two attempts, and who is a pretty good raid leader, wasn’t there for some reason, and I led the RZtW raid that time.  I’d like to think that I was the difference that made us win, but you just can’t toss aside time-honored expressions like “Third time’s a charm.”  And it was.  We’ve heard about guilds helping other guilds on the RZtW encounter, but this was really a cooperative venture.  Maybe even one guild was “more equal” than the other: out of 90 people on the raid, 32 were UoP.  Three days later, we came back and killed RZtW with 65 people.  We were flagged for the elemental planes!<br /><br />&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Out of all the times we’ve killed RZtW, we still haven’t gotten any Blades of War for our warriors.  Not one.  Bah.<br /><br />&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;After our RZtW kills, we opened applications and reflagged.  It was an interesting experience re-doing encounters with similar numbers that used to be difficult, but were now completely trivial.  You know what I mean.  Then we started farming elemental planes, and we’re still doing that now.  We started with Air, since the other guilds always got the good Fire and Water and Earth targets first, and then eventually got some chances at Earth rings, and then Water, and finally a Fire clear.  Finally, in late October, we got a chance at Fennin Ro and killed him on our first try.  I don’t know how disappointed I would have been if we didn’t win on the first try.  In the last month or so, we’ve killed Fennin Ro, Smith Rondo, Arbitor in Earth for EarthB keys, and Dust ring in Air for Xegony keys.  The Dust ring was the only real challenge for us, having to keep people awake and figuring out exactly how to deal with the spiders.  <br /><br />&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Right now, I’m running people through Tipt and Vxed for Kod`Taz flags when we aren’t raiding.  I’m the only active enchanter main in the guild, so it’s a lot of mountain trials for me.  It seems like the work never ends, and it actually never does.  I guess that’s how survival goes, or more accurately, how life goes.  We’ll get a Xegony attempt soon, I hope, and maybe we’ll have enough to start raiding in Ikkinz.  But who knows?  As people like to say, anything could happen.  <br />&nbsp;<br />&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;As an individual, I play because of the people.  As a guild leader, I play for the guild.  In a sense, it’s my job to make possible the type of shared memories and experiences that we reminisce about with our friends and by which we can remember those who are no longer with us.  And even though it’s truly the people that make this game what it is for us, a game worth playing day after day, it’s the experiences that make the guild what it is.  The unexpected bad turn happens?  We make the best of it.  Someone treats us unfairly?  We’ll fight it, but not fight fire with fire.  Opportunities knock?  We open the door.  Cooperate?  Always.  We never give up; never surrender.  Tomorrow’s another day.  And if all hope is lost and we cannot win, we will fight the long defeat.  What does that make us?  &lt;Apotheosis&gt;.<br /><br /><br /><br /><br /><br />(*) I’ve used the opening sentence of a book here.  I’ve substituted the appropriate guild-related words into it.  Chocolate ice cream to anyone who can name the book!<br />
						</td>
					</tr>
					<tr>
